The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions concerning Sudan, in particular resolutions 1591 (2005), 1651 (2005), 1665 (2006), 1672 (2006), 1713 (2006), 1779 (2007), 1841 (2008), 1891 (2009), 1945 (2010), 1982 (2011), 2035 (2012), 2091 (2013), 2138 (2014), 2200 (2015), 2265 (2016), 2340 (2017), 2400 (2018), 2455 (2019), 2508 (2020), 2562 (2021), 2620 (2022), 2676 (2023), 2725 (2024), 2736 (2024), and 2750 (2024), and its presidential statement of 11 December 2018 (S/PRST/2018/19), and its press statements,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, unity, independence, and territorial integrity of Sudan, and to the principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Expressing grave concern at the continued conflict in Sudan, which continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region, and at the devastating humanitarian situation, including famine conditions affecting hundreds of thousands of people and the displacement of millions within Sudan and across its borders, including into South Sudan and neighbouring countries,

Deeply alarmed by continued reports of attacks against civilians and civilian objects, including markets, hospitals, schools, water and power infrastructure, and internally displaced persons camps, in particular the attacks in Omdurman, El Fasher, Zamzam camp, and Gezira State, and by reports of unlawful killings, sexual and gender-based violence, enforced disappearances, torture, recruitment and use of children, and looting,

Condemning all violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, and recalling that attacks deliberately directed against civilians, civilian objects, medical personnel, and humanitarian personnel may constitute war crimes,

Stressing the obligation of all parties to the conflict to respect international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality, and precaution, and to ensure the safe and unhindered passage of humanitarian relief,

Reaffirming the primary responsibility of the Government of Sudan to protect civilians within its territory and to facilitate humanitarian access, while recalling that all parties to the conflict bear obligations under international humanitarian law,

Welcoming the efforts of the Secretary-General and his Personal Envoy for Sudan, the African Union, the Intergovernmental Authority on Development, and other regional and international partners to advance a negotiated and inclusive political process, and recalling the commitments made by the parties in the Jeddah Declaration,

Taking note of the Secretary-General’s reports, including on the humanitarian situation and on progress in the implementation of the benchmarks established in resolution 2676 (2023), and the findings of the Panel of Experts,

Reaffirming the measures imposed and renewed by resolution 2750 (2024), including the arms embargo and targeted sanctions related to Darfur, and emphasizing the importance of full compliance by all Member States and all parties,

Noting the importance of ensuring accountability for serious violations of international humanitarian law and serious violations and abuses of human rights law, and welcoming the work of the Independent International Fact-Finding Mission for the Sudan,

Determining that the situation in Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

1. Demands that all parties to the conflict in Sudan immediately cease hostilities and agree to a durable, comprehensive, and nationwide ceasefire, and calls upon them to return to negotiations in good faith without preconditions, including through the Jeddah process, the African Union, IGAD, and the good offices of the Secretary-General, with the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women, youth, and civil society;

2. Demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law and human rights law, protect civilians, and refrain from attacks against civilian objects, including medical facilities, schools, markets, water and electricity infrastructure, and internally displaced persons camps;

3. Condemns in the strongest terms the continuing attacks by the Rapid Support Forces and allied militias against civilians, including in El Fasher, Zamzam camp, Omdurman, and Gezira State, demands that the Rapid Support Forces immediately halt such attacks, cease the siege of populated areas, and withdraw from civilian areas, and calls upon the Sudanese Armed Forces to refrain from indiscriminate airstrikes and to ensure that its operations comply with international humanitarian law;

4. Demands that all parties facilitate safe, timely, unhindered, and sustained humanitarian access to all civilians in need throughout Sudan, without discrimination, and through all available routes, including cross-line and cross-border routes, and calls upon the Government of Sudan to remove bureaucratic impediments, expedite visas and permits, and cooperate fully with the United Nations and humanitarian partners;

5. Calls upon all parties to ensure the safety and security of humanitarian personnel, medical personnel, and journalists, and to protect humanitarian convoys, facilities, and supplies, and urges all Member States to support the United Nations humanitarian response plan for Sudan and the regional refugee response;

6. Condemns all violations and abuses of human rights and international humanitarian law, including sexual and gender-based violence and the recruitment and use of children, stresses the need to end impunity, calls upon all parties to cooperate with the Independent International Fact-Finding Mission for the Sudan and with relevant United Nations mechanisms, and underscores the importance of holding perpetrators accountable;

7. Calls upon all Member States to refrain from external interference that fuels the conflict, to respect the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Sudan, and to comply strictly with the arms embargo and other measures reaffirmed in resolution 2750 (2024), and expresses concern at reports of arms transfers and external support that contribute to the continuation and escalation of the conflict;

8. Reaffirms the measures imposed by resolution 2750 (2024) and its predecessors, stresses the importance of full implementation of the Panel of Experts’ recommendations, and expresses its readiness to consider additional measures, as appropriate, against individuals and entities who threaten the peace, security, or stability of Sudan, obstruct humanitarian access, or commit violations of international humanitarian law or human rights law;

9. Supports an inclusive, Sudanese-owned and Sudanese-led political process aimed at restoring a civilian-led transition and achieving lasting peace, and calls for close coordination among the United Nations, the African Union, IGAD, and other regional and international partners in support of such a process;

10. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 90 days on the implementation of this resolution, including on the humanitarian situation, access, protection of civilians, and compliance by the parties with the demands set out above, and decides to remain seized of the matter.

