7. Calls upon all Member States to refrain from external interference that fuels the conflict, to respect the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Sudan and South Sudan and to refrain from actions that could destabilize neighbouring States, and to comply strictly with the arms embargo and other measures reaffirmed in resolution 2750 (2024); urges all Member States to take concrete measures to prevent the transfer of arms and ammunition to the parties to the conflict and to report such measures to the 1591 Committee; expresses concern at reports of arms transfers and external support that contribute to the continuation and escalation of the conflict; and affirms that bilateral cooperation with the Government of Sudan should be consistent with international law and Council resolutions;

8. Reaffirms the measures imposed by resolution 2750 (2024) and its predecessors; stresses the importance of full implementation of the Panel of Experts’ recommendations; calls upon all Member States to comply strictly with the arms embargo and other measures; emphasizes that the 1591 Committee should continue to apply the existing listing criteria in a targeted manner and with fair and clear procedures consistent with resolution 2744 (2024), ensuring humanitarian exemptions and avoiding unintended humanitarian consequences; and requests the 1591 Committee, with the support of the Panel of Experts, to intensify its engagement with Member States on the implementation of the arms embargo, including through information-sharing and briefings;

9. Supports an inclusive, Sudanese-owned and Sudanese-led political process aimed at restoring a civilian-led transition and achieving lasting peace, and calls for close coordination among the United Nations, the African Union, IGAD, the Organization of Islamic Cooperation, and other regional and international partners in support of such a process;

10. Stresses that all United Nations and international efforts in support of Sudan must respect the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Sudan, be based on thorough consultations with the Government of Sudan, and fully respect Sudanese ownership, and calls for strengthened coordination with the African Union and IGAD; and requests the Secretary-General, in close consultation with the Government of Sudan, the African Union, IGAD and other regional stakeholders, to consult on and submit recommendations within 90 days on strengthening the protection of civilians in Sudan, building on existing mechanisms and consistent with resolution 2736 (2024);

11. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 90 days on the implementation of this resolution, including on the humanitarian situation, access and obstacles to access, protection of civilians, compliance by the parties with the demands set out above, and consultations with the Government of Sudan and regional organizations, and decides to remain seized of the matter.

